#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:1
msgid "Input-addressing derivation outputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:5
msgid ""
"\"Input addressing\" means the address the store object by the _way it was "
"made_ rather than _what it is_. That is to say, an input-addressed output's "
"store path is a function not of the output itself, but of the derivation "
"that produced it. Even if two store paths have the same contents, if they "
"are produced in different ways, and one is input-addressed, then they will "
"have different store paths, and thus guaranteed to not be the same store "
"object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:9
msgid "Masking content addressed derivation outputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:11
msgid ""
"A naive implementation of an output hash computation for input-addressed "
"outputs would be to hash the derivation hash and output together. This "
"clearly has the uniqueness properties we want for input-addressed outputs, "
"but suffers from an inefficiency. Specifically, new builds would be required "
"whenever a change is made to a fixed-output derivation, despite having "
"provably no differences in the inputs to the new derivation compared to what "
"it used to be. Concretely, this would cause a \"mass rebuild\" whenever any "
"fetching detail changes, including mirror lists, certificate authority "
"certificates, etc."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:16
msgid ""
"To solve this problem, we compute output hashes differently, so that certain "
"output hashes become identical. We do this by _masking_: before hashing, we "
"rewrite the parts of the derivation whose differences we do not want to see."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:19
msgid "There are two maskings, and they are independent:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:21
msgid ""
"_Input masking_ replaces each input derivation's store path with a hash "
"standing for what that input _produces_, rather than for the derivation that "
"produces it. This is what makes different fixed-output derivation inputs "
"that produce the same outputs stop mattering."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:24
msgid ""
"_Output masking_ blanks the derivation's own output paths --- in `outputs`, "
"and in the environment variables named after them. This is needed when those "
"very paths are what we are computing; it is not wanted when the derivation "
"is merely being hashed to stand in for an input to something else, where the "
"paths it already has are exactly what identifies it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:27
msgid ""
"A derivation with only the first applied is an _input-masked derivation_; "
"one with both is a _fully masked derivation_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:29
msgid ""
"Every non-injective function induces an equivalence relation on its domain, "
"by saying two elements are equivalent if they map to the same value. But the "
"equivalence relation induced by input masking is also pleasant to "
"characterize directly, without reference to input masking at all. "
"Intuitively this makes sense, because the details of how exactly inputs are "
"hashed (which hash function, say) shouldn't matter (ignoring hash collisions "
"and the like) --- all that matters is which derivations are deemed "
"equivalent. The [semantic properties](#semantic-properties) section below "
"develops this further."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:34
msgid ""
"So how do we compute the [hash part](../../../store/store-path.md#digest) of "
"the output paths of an input-addressed derivation? This is done by the "
"function `hashInputMaskedDerivation`, shown below, which does the input "
"masking itself; its callers do any output masking."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:37
msgid ""
"First, a word on inputs. `hashInputMaskedDerivation` is only defined on "
"derivations whose [inputs](../../../store/derivation/index.md#inputs) take "
"the first-order form:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:54
msgid ""
"For the algorithm below, we adopt a derivation where the two types of (first "
"order) derived paths are partitioned into two sets, as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:55
msgid ""
"```typescript\n"
"type Derivation = {\n"
"  // inputs: Set<FirstOrderDerivingPath>; // replaced\n"
"  inputSrcs: Set<ConstantPath>; // new instead\n"
"  inputDrvOutputs: Set<FirstOrderOutputPath>; // new instead\n"
"  // ...other fields...\n"
"};\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:64
msgid ""
"In the [currently-experimental](../../../development/experimental-features."
"md#xp-feature-dynamic-derivations) higher-order case where outputs of "
"outputs are allowed as [deriving paths](../../../store/derivation/index."
"md#deriving-path) and thus derivation inputs, derivations using that "
"generalization are not valid arguments to this function. Those derivations "
"must be (partially) [resolved](../../../store/resolution.md) enough first, "
"to the point where no such higher-order inputs remain. Then, and only then, "
"can input addresses be assigned."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:68
msgid ""
"```\n"
"function hashInputMaskedDerivation(drv) -> Hash:\n"
"    assert(drv.outputs are input-addressed)\n"
"    drv′ ← drv with {\n"
"        inputDrvOutputs = ⋃(\n"
"            assert(drvPath is store path)\n"
"            case hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation(readDrv(drvPath)) of\n"
"                drvHash : Hash →\n"
"                    (drvHash.toBase16(), output)\n"
"                outputHashes : Map[String, Hash] →\n"
"                    (outputHashes[output].toBase16(), \"out\")\n"
"            | (drvPath, output) ∈ drv.inputDrvOutputs\n"
"        )\n"
"    }\n"
"    return hashSHA256(printDrv(drv′))\n"
"\n"
"function hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation(drv) -> Map[String, Hash] | "
"Hash:\n"
"    if drv.outputs are content-addressed:\n"
"        return {\n"
"            outputName ↦ hashSHA256(\n"
"                \"fixed:out:\" + ca.printMethodAlgo() +\n"
"                \":\" + ca.hash.toBase16() +\n"
"                \":\" + ca.makeFixedOutputPath(drv.name, outputName))\n"
"            | (outputName ↦ output) ∈ drv.outputs\n"
"            , ca = output.contentAddress // or get from build trace if "
"floating\n"
"        }\n"
"    else: // drv.outputs are input-addressed\n"
"        return hashInputMaskedDerivation(drv)\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:98
msgid "`hashInputMaskedDerivation`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:100
msgid ""
"We replace each element in the derivation's `inputDrvOutputs` using data "
"from a call to `hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` on the `drvPath` of that "
"element. When `hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` returns a single drv hash "
"(because the input derivation in question is input-addressing), we simply "
"swap out the `drvPath` for that hash, and keep the same output name. When "
"`hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` returns a map of content addresses per-"
"output, we look up the output in question, and pair it with the output name "
"`out`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:104
msgid ""
"That rewriting is the input masking. The resulting pseudo-derivation (with "
"hashes instead of store paths in `inputDrvs`) is then printed (in the "
"[\"ATerm\" format](../../../protocols/derivation-aterm.md#input-address-"
"encoding)) and hashed, and this becomes the hash of the input-masked "
"derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:107
msgid ""
"When calculating output hashes, `hashInputMaskedDerivation` is called on an "
"almost-complete input-addressing derivation, which is just missing its input-"
"addressed outputs paths --- that is, one that is already output masked, "
"making it fully masked. The derivation hash is then used to calculate output "
"paths for each output, as the [complete store path calculation](../../../"
"protocols/store-path.md#fingerprint) specifies: it is the digest of the "
"hashed ATerm that specification calls for, under the `\"output:\" id` case, "
"where `id` is the output name. The store object's name is the derivation's "
"name, with `-` and the output name appended --- unless the output is named "
"`out`, in which case the derivation's name is used as-is."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:111
msgid ""
"Note that one derivation hash serves every output; what distinguishes their "
"paths is only the output name, which enters twice over, as both `id` and "
"part of the name."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:113
msgid ""
"Those output paths can then be substituted into the almost-complete input-"
"addressed derivation to complete it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:115
#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:130
#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:139
#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:169
#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:257
msgid "**Note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:117
msgid ""
"There may be an unintentional deviation from specification currently "
"implemented in the `(outputHashes[output].toBase16(), \"out\")` case. This "
"is not fatal because the deviation would only apply for content-addressing "
"derivations with more than one output, and that only occurs in the floating "
"case, which is [experimental](../../../development/experimental-features."
"md#xp-feature-ca-derivations). Once this bug is fixed, this note will be "
"removed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:121
msgid "`hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:123
msgid ""
"`hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` consists of two main cases, based on "
"whether the outputs of the derivation are to be input-addressed or content-"
"addressed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:125
msgid "Input-addressed outputs case"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:127
msgid ""
"In the input-addressed case, it just calls `hashInputMaskedDerivation`, and "
"returns that derivation hash. This makes `hashInputMaskedDerivation` and "
"`hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` mutually-recursive."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:132
msgid ""
"In this case, `hashInputMaskedDerivation` is being called on a _complete_ "
"input-addressing derivation that already has its output paths calculated: it "
"is not output masked, so it is input-masked only, not fully masked. The "
"input masking takes place anyways."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:135
msgid "Content-addressed outputs case"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:137
msgid ""
"If the outputs are [content-addressed](./content-address.md), then it "
"computes a hash for each output derived from the content-address of that "
"output."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:141
msgid ""
"In the [fixed](./content-address.md#fixed) content-addressing case, the "
"outputs' content addresses are statically specified in advance, so this "
"always just works. (The fixed case is what the pseudo-code shows.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:144
msgid ""
"In the [floating](./content-address.md#floating) case, the content addresses "
"are not specified in advance. This is what the \"or get from [build trace]"
"(../../../store/build-trace.md) if floating\" comment refers to. In this "
"case, the algorithm is _stuck_ until the input in question is built, and we "
"know what the actual contents of the output in question is."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:148
msgid ""
"That is OK however, because there is no problem with delaying the assigning "
"of input addresses (which, remember, is what `hashInputMaskedDerivation` is "
"ultimately for) until all inputs are known."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:150
msgid "Deferring input-addressing when downstream of unknown CA store objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:152
msgid ""
"As the previous note says, the algorithm is _stuck_ when an input is a "
"[floating content-addressed](./content-address.md#floating) output that has "
"not been built yet: there is no content address to substitute for it, so the "
"input masking cannot be completed and no input address can be computed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:154
msgid ""
"The derivation is still written to the store, with its outputs _deferred_: "
"an output in this state has no store path at all, and the environment "
"variable named after it is empty. This costs nothing, because a derivation's "
"own store path is [content-addressed](../../../store/store-object/content-"
"address.md#method-text) on the bytes of its serialization, which do not "
"depend on its output paths being known."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:157
msgid ""
"A deferred output does not use a third kind of addressing alongside input- "
"and content-addressing. It is input-addressed; it just does not know its "
"path yet."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:160
msgid ""
"Being stuck is contagious. A derivation with deferred outputs has, for this "
"purpose, unknown outputs of its own, so `hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` "
"is stuck on it in turn, and anything depending on it must also defer. The "
"condition therefore propagates downstream from the floating output that "
"caused it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:164
msgid ""
"Deferral is resolved by building, not by revisiting the same derivation. "
"Once the offending inputs are built, their content addresses are known, and "
"the derivation can be [resolved](../../../store/resolution.md): its inputs "
"are replaced by the plain store paths they turned out to denote. The "
"resolved derivation is a _different_ derivation, with its own store path --- "
"necessarily so, since it has different bytes. It has no input derivations "
"left to be stuck on, so its input addresses are computable, and it is what "
"actually gets built."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:171
msgid ""
"Nothing gates deferred outputs themselves. They only _arise_ downstream of "
"[floating content-addressed](./content-address.md#floating) outputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:174
msgid "Performance"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:176
msgid ""
"The recursion in the algorithm is potentially inefficient: it could call "
"itself once for each path by which a subderivation can be reached, i.e., "
"`O(V^k)` times for a derivation graph with `V` derivations and with out-"
"degree of at most `k`. In the actual implementation, [memoisation](https://"
"en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memoization) is used to reduce this cost to be "
"proportional to the total number of `inputDrvOutputs` encountered."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:180
msgid "Semantic properties"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:182
msgid ""
"_See [this chapter's appendix](../../../store/math-notation.md) on grammar "
"and metavariable conventions._"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:184
msgid ""
"Masking is, mathematically, a quotient, in reference to quotient types or "
"sets: what it discards is exactly what we want two derivations to be allowed "
"to differ in. Accordingly, `hashInputMaskedDerivation` partitions input-"
"addressing derivations into equivalence classes: every derivation in that "
"equivalence class is mapped to the same derivation hash. We can characterize "
"this equivalence relation directly, by working bottom up."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:188
msgid ""
"We start by defining an equivalence relation on first-order output deriving "
"paths that refer content-addressed derivation outputs. Two such paths are "
"equivalent if they refer to the same store object:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:190
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$d\\_1$ is content-addressing} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$d\\_2$ is content-addressing} \\\\AxiomC{$ {}^\\*(\\text{path}"
"(d\\_1), o\\_1) = {}^\\*(\\text{path}(d\\_2), o\\_2) $} \\"
"\\TrinaryInfC{$(\\text{path}(d\\_1), o\\_1) \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\\\mathrm{CA}}\\"
"\\, (d\\_2, o\\_2)$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:203
msgid ""
"where \\\\({}^\\*(s, o)\\\\) denotes the store object that the output "
"deriving path refers to."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:205
msgid ""
"We will also need the following construction to lift any equivalence "
"relation on \\\\(X\\\\) to an equivalence relation on (finite) sets of \\"
"\\(X\\\\) (in short, \\\\(\\\\mathcal{P}(X)\\\\)):"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:207
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$\\\\forall a \\\\in A. \\\\exists b "
"\\\\in B. a \\\\,\\\\sim\\_X\\\\, b$} \\\\AxiomC{$\\\\forall b \\\\in B. \\"
"\\exists a \\\\in A. b \\\\,\\\\sim\\_X\\\\, a$} \\\\BinaryInfC{$A \\\\,\\"
"\\sim\\_{\\\\mathcal{P}(X)}\\\\, B$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:215
msgid ""
"Now we can define the equivalence relation \\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{IA}\\"
"\\) on input-addressed derivation outputs. Two input-addressed outputs are "
"equivalent if their derivations are equivalent (via the yet-to-be-defined \\"
"\\(\\\\sim\\_{\\\\mathrm{IADrv}}\\\\) relation) and their output names are "
"the same:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:217
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$d\\_1$ is input-addressing} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$d\\_2$ is input-addressing} \\\\AxiomC{$d\\_1 \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\"
"\\mathrm{IADrv}}\\\\, d\\_2$} \\\\AxiomC{$o\\_1 = o\\_2$} \\"
"\\QuaternaryInfC{$(\\text{path}(d\\_1), o\\_1) \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\"
"\\mathrm{IA}}\\\\, (\\text{path}(d\\_2), o\\_2)$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:227
msgid ""
"And now we can define \\\\(\\\\sim\\_{\\\\mathrm{IADrv}}\\\\). Two input-"
"addressed derivations are equivalent if their content-addressed inputs are "
"equivalent, their input-addressed inputs are also equivalent, and they are "
"otherwise equal:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:232
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\alwaysNoLine \\\\AxiomC{$ \\"
"\\mathrm{caInputs}(d\\_1) \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\\\mathcal{P}(\\\\mathrm{CA})}\\"
"\\, \\\\mathrm{caInputs}(d\\_2) $} \\\\AxiomC{$ \\\\mathrm{iaInputs}(d\\_1) "
"\\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\\\mathcal{P}(\\\\mathrm{IA})}\\\\, \\\\mathrm{iaInputs}"
"(d\\_2) $} \\\\BinaryInfC{$ d\\_1\\left\\[\\\\mathrm{inputDrvOutputs} := \\"
"\\{\\\\}\\right\\] = d\\_2\\left\\[\\\\mathrm{inputDrvOutputs} := \\\\{\\"
"\\}\\right\\] $} \\\\alwaysSingleLine \\\\UnaryInfC{$d\\_1 \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\"
"\\mathrm{IADrv}}\\\\, d\\_2$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:255
msgid ""
"where \\\\(\\\\mathrm{caInputs}(d)\\\\) returns the content-addressed inputs "
"of \\\\(d\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\mathrm{iaInputs}(d)\\\\) returns the input-"
"addressed inputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:259
msgid ""
"An astute reader might notice that that nowhere does `inputSrcs` enter into "
"these definitions. That means that replacing an input derivation with its "
"outputs directly added to `inputSrcs` always results in a derivation in a "
"different equivalence class, despite the resulting input closure (as would "
"be mounted in the store at build time) being the same. [Issue #9259](https://"
"github.com/NixOS/nix/issues/9259) is about creating a coarser equivalence "
"relation to address this."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:263
msgid ""
"\\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\) from [derivation resolution](../../../"
"store/resolution.md) is such an equivalence relation. It is coarser than "
"this one: any two derivations which are \"'hash input-masked derivation'-"
"equivalent\" (\\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{IADrv}\\\\)) are also \"resolution-"
"equivalent\" (\\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\)). It also relates "
"derivations whose `inputDrvOutputs` have been rewritten into `inputSrcs`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:267
msgid ""
"Input-addressing downstream of content addressing, as described above, "
"anticipates this. Since a content-addressed input contributes only its "
"content address, and not the derivation that produced it, derivations "
"differing only in _how_ an identical content-addressed input was produced "
"are already \\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{IADrv}\\\\)-equivalent. And since a "
"deferred derivation is completely resolved before it is input-addressed, its "
"input-addressed `inputDrvOutputs` are normalized to `inputSrcs` as well --- "
"so derivations differing only in how an identical _input-addressed_ input "
"was produced are \\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{IADrv}\\\\)-equivalent too. That "
"is the same \"identify an input by what it is, rather than by how it was "
"made\" move as \\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\). The difference is that \\"
"\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\) makes it unconditionally, whereas here it "
"falls out for content-addressed inputs always, and for the rest only where "
"deferral forced a resolution first."
msgstr ""
